00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:27.750
This is Tech in 2. The topic for today is
backend development, so let's start that

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.439
timer! So in order to talk about back in
development, let's revisit what front-end

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:35.579
development is: it's the practice of
creating user interfaces that are

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:40.140
flexible and make it easy for a user to
interact with data. Backend development

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.610
is a practice of creating those plugs
into the data, oftentimes called an

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:49.680
application program interface or API. I
honestly like to think of the backend as

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.110
like a panel of plugs, where each plug
returns a massaged subset of the data, and

00:00:55.110 --> 00:01:00.719
the panel itself hides away all the
complexity of fetching from and updating to

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:03.480
the database.
Unlike front-end development, back-end

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.909
isn't specific to any one platform; in
fact it serves as more like a foundation

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:11.820
that supports whatever platforms sit on
top of it.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.770
So to illustrate this, let's go back to
our ATM analogy. So when a user wants to

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.580
withdraw $40 from their account,
they'll tap on various buttons on the

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.650
machine (or our UI in this case) that then
communicates to the backend to actually

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.939
carry out those tasks. Now let's go back
to the panel of plugs visualization.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:34.650
Imagine the front-end plugged into one
plug called "get money". That plug actually

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:38.610
connects to several other plugs on the
wall to do the business logic - for

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.840
instance, one to check that the user
actually has $40 in their account, and

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.189
the other to subtract that amount from
their balance. Developing each of those

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:51.509
plugs is back-end development to make
sure that the user interacts with the

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:56.250
data safely. So what's in the stack? Well
usually it involves two types of

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.329
languages: one is a server-side
programming language that helps build

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:06.430
the API itself - commonly used are Python
Java Ruby PHP and javascript.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:10.179
And the second is a SQL
language to actually fetch the data from

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:13.780
the database. And what are the important
factors and skills for working in

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.620
back-end development? One is to have the
architectural skills for making

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.510
well-organized, extensible, and testable
code. A big part of back-end is

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.550
organizing logic and data ,so those
skills allow for writing code with speed

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.480
and quality. And the second is to
communicate clearly with front-end

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.140
developers - there's a myth that back-end
developers don't need to know anything

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.640
about users (which is wrong by the way).
Back-end developers are a big part of

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.600
allowing users do what they need to do
to get done, so it's a joint effort

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.160
between front-end developers and
back-end developers to write code to do

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.510
just this. And that is back-end
development. And that's it for this

